  "title": "TNS-aware van Ginneken: carry per-option total sink violation and choose the lowest-TNS tree among those within epsilon of the best worst-sink slack",
  "hypothesis": "Rebuffer::rebufferNet scores buffer-tree options only by (load cap, earliest required time) and picks the root option with the largest worst-sink slack, so on shared nets with many violating sinks it ignores total violation. We add a third Pareto dimension V (sum of sink violation, propagated piecewise-linearly through wires/buffers via a violating-sink count and summed at junctions) and choose, among root options within epsilon=min(1ps, 5%|S*|) of the stock-best slack, the one with the lowest V. This captures the shared-endpoint TNS benefit seen in iterations 8/9 locally per net, without the global target reordering that broke AES/JPEG, and without the eligibility expansion (iter 6) or split-partition prediction (iter 7) dead ends. With at most one violating sink it reduces exactly to stock. Expected: Ibex/JPEG TNS improves on shared-net cones while WNS stays within 1 ps.",
